<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ionian University of Smyrna</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1920">1920</a>–<a href="/years/1922">1922</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ionian University of Smyrna was a Greek university planned in Smyrna during the Greek administration of the city after 1919. The mathematician <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantin-caratheodory">Constantin Carathéodory</a> was appointed to organise it in 1920, and buildings, staff and a library were prepared, but it never opened: the Greek army's defeat and the Turkish capture of Smyrna in September 1922 ended the project.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ionian-university-of-smyrna.jpg" alt="Ionian University of Smyrna" width="320" height="185" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ionian_University_of_Smyrna.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Greek education was already well established in Smyrna under Ottoman rule: in the early 20th century 67 schools were supported by the local Greek community, the church and private bodies, the best known being the Homer School for Girls, the Graeco-German Lycee and the Evangelical School, which had an archaeological museum, a natural science collection and a library. A university was one of the projects closest to the heart of the Greek High Commissioner, Aristidis Stergiadis. The mathematician <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantin-caratheodory">Constantin Carathéodory</a>, then a professor at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-gottingen">University of Göttingen</a>, came to run it at the request of the Greek government.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In September 1920 Georgios Ioakimoglou, a lecturer in pharmacology at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humboldt-university-of-berlin">Humboldt University of Berlin</a>, joined Carathéodory to help organise the project and to hold the chair of hygiene and microbiology. The Greek administration paid for the foundation and gave the university a large, half-finished building begun in 1911 for the School of Union and Progress by Mustafa Rahmi Bey, governor of the Aydin Vilayet. A decree of the High Commissioner proclaimed the university's foundation in December 1920, following a memorandum by Carathéodory.</p><p>Four schools were planned: agriculture and natural sciences, oriental languages, social and economic sciences, and trade and the supervision of building work. The School of Agriculture was to advise landowners and farmers on cultivation and on plant and animal diseases, working with an experimental farm at Tepekoy that impressed the historian Arnold J. Toynbee, then the Manchester Guardian's correspondent. Teaching was to be in Greek, with Turkish or other languages in some cases, and admission was to be open regardless of sex or nationality. On 11 May 1921 the High Commissioner's Office approved 18,000 Swiss francs for the library, which Carathéodory organised on European lines with help from a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a> catalogue agency.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The Institute of Hygiene and the library were due to open on 10 October 1922, but the evacuation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> came first. Carathéodory managed to send the material meant for Smyrna to the new Central Library of Athens University. From 1923 the building housed the İzmir Teachers' College for Males, and in 1936, after the college moved to Buca, it was given to İzmir Girls' High School.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ionian University of Smyrna” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A Greek university, the <b>Ionian University</b>, was planned under the mathematician Constantin Caratheodory.</blockquote><a href="/state/smyrna-zone">Smyrna Zone</a></li></ul></section>
